{"qid":"Q187684","names":["Despotate of Epirus"],"title":"Despotate of Epirus","endonym":{"native":"Δεσποτάτο της Ηπείρου","lang":"el"},"capsule":"The Despotate of Epirus was a Byzantine successor state founded c. 1204–1205 by Michael I Komnenos Doukas after the Latin sack of Constantinople, governing western Greece and Albania from its capital at Arta. Under Theodore Komnenos Doukas (r. 1215–1230) it reached its greatest extent, absorbing Thessaloniki in 1224 and claiming the Byzantine imperial title, until Theodore was defeated and captured by the Bulgarian tsar Ivan Asen II at the Battle of Klokotnitsa (1230). Thereafter the despotate contracted and fragmented, passing to the Orsini family and then to the Byzantine Palaiologoi; it finally fell when the Ottoman sultan Mehmed II absorbed the last remnant under Leonardo III Tocco in 1479, with Arta itself taken in 1449.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The core territory of Epirus and Thessaly is well-documented in Byzantine chronicles and lead seals, though the despotate's northern and eastern boundaries were contested and shifted frequently.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Despotate of Epirus","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Despotate-of-Epirus"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Despotate of Epirus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Despotate_of_Epirus"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Theodore Komnenos Doukas","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodore_Komnenos_Doukas"}],"britannica":"place/despotate-of-Epirus","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Despotate of Epirus","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Michael I Komnenos Doukas","nativeName":"Μιχαήλ Α' Κομν